title: Labels and contexts
tags: [[The operation log]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
status: proposed
summary: Operations are grouped by nested labels (name, description, parent) opened as contexts by scripts, commands and the GUI — passed along as an id, so leaving a context is just not passing it on.

A ''label'' is `{id, name, description, parent, created}`. Each operation carries its
''innermost'' label; the others are its ancestors, found by following the parents. Same
information as every label on every operation while contexts nest, without copying three
ids onto each of a bulk write's 50 000 operations.

!! Contexts

A context is a label that the writes made inside it carry. It is ''not daemon state'':

* `mf label begin "name" "description"` creates a label, child of the current context, and prints its id;
* the caller exports it (`METAFOLDER_LABEL=<id>`), and every `mf` started meanwhile sends it with its requests;
* leaving the context is ceasing to export it — the end of a subshell or a function's `local`. A script that crashes leaves nothing open behind it.

Every command opens its own label, a child of the context: `mf metarecord -q '…' field
set …` is a label whose description is the command line and its query, so the log shows
what was asked, not only what was written.

!! Who opens which

* A shipped tagging script: one label for the run, one per answer (its going back is reverting the last answer's label — no head noted by hand, no rollback), one per command inside.
* The GUI: one label per command. A script launched from the GUI inherits the context of the command that launched it.
* The GUI's ''work context'': a command opens a context that every later user operation carries until it is closed — naming the work under way, so it can be looked at or reverted whole. The watcher's operations do not carry it.
* The watcher: one label per flush, under a ''watcher root'' that only the daemon can write under. The root a label descends from is its ''origin'' — what `revision.origin` said, and what undo and retention read. As today, a client cannot pass its writes off as the daemon's.

!! The log panel

Operations grouped by their outermost label (name and description); a group opens onto
its child labels, down to single operations. Consecutive watcher labels are folded into
one line, or they would drown the rest.

!! Deferred

The GUI's work context is secondary (user, 12 October 2026), and not part of the first
build: whether it belongs to a workspace or to the whole GUI, and whether it survives a
restart, is decided when it is built. So is renaming or describing a label after the fact.
